<?php

return [
    ['gravure-pouch-brief', 'A better rotogravure pouch brief starts before the artwork.', 'rotogravure-pouches', 'blog-roto-brief',
        'Separate the product, structure and print decisions to make a pouch inquiry easier to review.',
        [
            'Describe what the pouch must do' => 'A printed pouch is often judged first by its front panel. A useful buying brief starts earlier: what is inside, how it will be filled, where it will be stored and how the customer will open it. Write those answers before choosing a laminate or a finish. A photograph can communicate the desired appearance, but it cannot establish material compatibility or product protection.',
            'Separate structure from printing' => 'Rotogravure describes the printing route. Stand-up, three-side-seal and centre-seal describe different pack structures. Keep these decisions in separate lines of the brief so that a change to one does not silently change the other. If you prefer a standing format, explain whether a zipper or a window is essential, optional or simply an idea to evaluate.',
            'Identify the artwork versions' => 'Prepare a simple register containing the product name, file identifier, revision and quantity for each design. Mark which files are approved and which are references. Cylinder preparation and repeat-order planning depend on a stable understanding of the artwork. Do not let an attractive mock-up become the approval record for small text or regulatory information.',
            'Make the quote comparable' => 'Send the same dimensions, quantities, material requirements and approval expectations when comparing quotations. Ask whether tooling, proofs, delivery and taxes are included, without assuming a particular supplier policy. For an Indian business purchase, include the commercial information needed for the quotation through a private discussion; do not place tax identifiers or private documents in public comments.',
            'Review the filled pack' => 'A flat design and a filled pouch are different views of the same project. Check where the fill line sits, whether branding remains visible and how the opening area works. Ask which samples or other validation are appropriate for the product. Keep the accepted specification with the approved artwork so a repeat order can refer to both.',
            'Bring a decision-ready brief' => 'The practical goal is a clear record of the product, proposed format, artwork versions, quantity and unresolved questions. Explore the rotogravure service and its format guides, then use the contact form to explain your requirements. Final structure, availability and commercial details should be confirmed before an order is agreed.',
        ],
        'Must I choose a material before making an inquiry?', 'No. Describe the product and the performance questions you need to resolve. A proposed structure can then be evaluated against that brief.',
        ['Print planning', 'Pouch specifications']],
    ['gravure-repeat-order-colour', 'Planning repeat pouch orders without losing control of the print.', 'rotogravure-pouches', 'blog-roto-repeat',
        'A practical approval record connects repeat artwork, colour references and the finished pack.',
        [
            'Define what repeat means' => 'A repeat order should identify a specific approved specification. A product name alone is not enough if the same product has appeared in several sizes, artwork versions or material structures. Start the order record with the accepted artwork identifier, dimensions, structure and the date of the relevant approval.',
            'Keep colour references intentional' => 'Screen appearance varies with the device and viewing conditions. Describe which physical reference or agreed proof is to guide colour assessment and which details need special attention. A coloured product photograph is helpful for context but should not be treated as an exact colour standard. Where a finish changes the look, include it in the approval discussion.',
            'Control small changes' => 'A revised ingredient line, a new product weight or a shifted graphic can be significant even when the main design looks unchanged. Record each change and the party responsible for approving it. Ask how the change affects the print setup, existing tooling and the order. Avoid assuming that a small artwork edit is automatically a small production change.',
            'Check the structure as well as the image' => 'A different material face, window arrangement or seal layout may alter the visual result. If the requirement has changed, issue a revised brief rather than calling the order an identical repeat. Review the standing shape, readable information and the relationship between the main design and seals for the chosen pouch format.',
            'Maintain an approval trail' => 'Use one controlled location for approved artwork and specification files. Mark obsolete versions clearly and avoid sending several competing files labelled final. The record should distinguish the artwork approval from any product-performance or commercial approval. Each has a different purpose and may involve different people.',
            'Close the loop before reordering' => 'A repeat-order checklist can stay short: correct product, current artwork, accepted specification, colour reference, quantity and open changes. Use the rotogravure guide to compare relevant pouch structures, and contact Amalez PackTrade with the approved reference and the changes you need reviewed. A clear record supports a better conversation without promising an identical result under every condition.',
        ],
        'Is a phone photograph an exact colour approval?', 'No. Agree an appropriate proof or physical reference and the assessment conditions for the project.',
        ['Artwork approval', 'Repeat orders']],
    ['digital-pouch-multiple-designs', 'One product family, several pouch designs: organise the digital brief.', 'digital-printed-pouches', 'blog-digital-versions',
        'Build a version matrix that makes design changes and quantities visible before approval.',
        [
            'Start with a version matrix' => 'Digital printed pouches can be considered when several designs or artwork changes are central to a project. Before discussing the print route, list each product version and its quantity. A shared total quantity can hide an important difference between one large design and several smaller versions. Keep the commercial brief explicit.',
            'Separate shared and changing elements' => 'Mark the brand position, pack size and structural requirements that are intended to stay common. Then list the text, colour, image or other artwork that changes. This simple separation helps a reviewer spot whether a graphic change also brings a new fill quantity, different product behaviour or a material question.',
            'Name the files for the people using them' => 'Use an understandable product identifier and revision in every artwork filename. Match those identifiers to the version matrix. Keep reference mock-ups separate from print artwork and label unapproved files as drafts. The right file is more useful than a folder containing many vaguely named alternatives.',
            'Approve each version on its own merits' => 'A master layout can create consistency, but each variant still needs a content check. Product information, instructions and any regulated wording must be approved by the responsible business team. Do not approve one version and assume that every changed line in the others has been reviewed.',
            'Keep the physical requirements visible' => 'A digital print route does not decide the pouch closure, seal arrangement or material suitability. Confirm those requirements for the actual contents. Where all versions share a structure, say so explicitly; where they differ, provide a separate line in the brief. The aim is clarity rather than an artificially uniform range.',
            'Move from matrix to inquiry' => 'Send the design count, quantity per version, approved file identifiers and the unresolved structural questions. Explore the digital stand-up and multi-design format guides before making the inquiry. Amalez PackTrade can review the requirement without relying on a universal minimum order or lead-time claim.',
        ],
        'Does one shared design template count as approval of all variants?', 'No. Each version needs its own content and artwork review, including changed product information.',
        ['Digital artwork', 'Version control']],
    ['digital-pouch-proof-approval', 'What to check when approving digitally printed pouch artwork.', 'digital-printed-pouches', 'blog-digital-approval',
        'Distinguish a layout check from a material, colour or filled-pack assessment.',
        [
            'Ask what the proof represents' => 'A proof may show the artwork layout, a colour reference or a physical print on a particular material. Those are different forms of evidence. Ask which decisions the supplied proof is intended to support and which remain open. A clear answer prevents a layout approval from being mistaken for a full packaging-performance approval.',
            'Read the actual-size information' => 'Review small text at the intended size and check the front, back and opening orientation. Make sure important wording does not sit in the seal allowance or a fold. Zooming in on a screen can make weak legibility appear acceptable, so include a sensible actual-size review in the process.',
            'Examine the material and background' => 'Artwork on white paper will not necessarily look the same on transparent, white or metallic flexible material. Discuss the areas intended to be opaque and those that should remain visible. If a finish is part of the brief, understand whether the sample represents it or only approximates the visual idea.',
            'Check content separately from appearance' => 'An attractive design can still carry an obsolete product name or an incorrect instruction. Use a content checklist that identifies who approves the wording, dimensions and final artwork. Technical, regulatory and brand decisions should not be silently delegated to whoever happens to receive the final email.',
            'Keep a clear approval record' => 'State the file identifier, revision and scope of approval. Record any conditions or unresolved issues in plain language. If the artwork changes after approval, issue a new controlled version and review the affected details. Avoid combining an approval with unrelated comments that make the decision ambiguous.',
            'Finish with the complete brief' => 'A sound handover links the approved artwork to the intended pouch structure, material discussion, quantities and packing method. Explore the digital flat and stand-up guides for structural questions and share the remaining points through the contact form. A proof is a useful step, not a substitute for agreeing the whole project.',
        ],
        'Does approving a mock-up confirm product compatibility?', 'No. Product compatibility and packaging performance require their own review and, where appropriate, testing.',
        ['Print proofs', 'Artwork approval']],
    ['flexo-label-material-adhesive', 'A label is a system: face material, adhesive and container.', 'flexo-labels', 'blog-flexo-material',
        'Choose label requirements from the application outward, rather than from appearance alone.',
        [
            'Begin with the container' => 'A label interacts with the surface beneath it. Identify the container material, shape, rigidity and surface condition before choosing a label by its visual finish. A rigid glass jar and a squeezable plastic bottle create different questions, even when the desired artwork is identical.',
            'Describe the environment in two stages' => 'Application conditions and service conditions are separate parts of the brief. Explain the state of the container when the label is applied, then describe storage and use afterwards. Moisture, temperature changes, handling and exposure should be stated as real conditions rather than condensed into a vague request for a durable label.',
            'Compare appearance without assuming performance' => 'Paper, white film and clear film can support different design intentions. A clear face may reveal the container, while white material establishes an opaque background. These appearance choices do not by themselves specify adhesive performance, resistance or the behaviour of the complete pack.',
            'State removal and movement requirements' => 'Should the label remain attached for the product life, or is removal part of the intended use? Does the container flex or squeeze? Give those details to the reviewer. An adhesive selected only by a broad label category may miss the practical conditions that matter on the actual container.',
            'Review an applied sample' => 'Look at the label after application on a representative filled container. Check edges, bubbles, wrinkles, readability and any movement during handling. Decide which observations need a formal acceptance criterion and which are visual preferences. If a resistance claim is required, ask what evidence is appropriate instead of relying on the material name.',
            'Make the selection traceable' => 'Keep the accepted material construction, container reference and application conditions with the approved artwork. Explore the paper and filmic label guides to frame the comparison. Amalez PackTrade focuses the inquiry on the complete label requirement without claiming that one stock suits every product.',
        ],
        'Is a film label automatically waterproof?', 'Do not assume that. The face material, adhesive, print, finish and actual exposure need assessment as a complete label construction.',
        ['Label materials', 'Adhesive selection']],
    ['flexo-label-roll-handover', 'Before ordering roll labels, confirm the application details.', 'flexo-labels', 'blog-flexo-application',
        'The roll, liner and unwind arrangement matter alongside the printed face.',
        [
            'Describe how the label will be applied' => 'A roll label must work with the intended application process. State whether application is by hand or with equipment, and identify the relevant equipment requirements through the responsible operator. A correctly printed label can still be inconvenient or unsuitable if the roll arrangement is wrong.',
            'Use a dimensioned layout' => 'Include the label width, height, shape and the direction used to describe them. The gap and layout on the liner should be agreed where relevant. A screenshot of the artwork is not a dependable substitute for the dimensioned arrangement used by the applicator.',
            'Agree the unwind view' => 'Top-first, bottom-first, left-first and right-first descriptions can become ambiguous when people imagine different viewing positions. Use a labelled drawing or the equipment operator’s accepted convention. Confirm whether the print faces inward or outward and which edge reaches the application point first.',
            'Confirm roll requirements' => 'Ask the operator for the relevant core, maximum roll size, winding and handling requirements. Record these separately from the quantity of labels. Avoid guessing a standard roll simply because it worked for a previous project; the new label size or container may change the practical arrangement.',
            'Keep printed and variable information distinct' => 'If the pack needs a batch code, date or other variable information, identify how and when it will be added. Confirm the designated area and the intended overprinting method with the responsible team. A blank area in a design does not establish compatibility with every coding process.',
            'Hand over one clear record' => 'Combine the approved artwork, label construction, dimensioned layout and roll requirements into a controlled brief. The clear and white film guides can help with appearance questions, while the inquiry should explain the actual container and application conditions. Final details need confirmation before an order is placed.',
        ],
        'Can unwind direction be agreed using text alone?', 'A clear drawing or the operator’s established convention is safer, especially when the label orientation is easy to interpret in different ways.',
        ['Roll labels', 'Application planning']],
    ['paper-box-dieline-artwork', 'From flat artwork to a paper box: read the dieline first.', 'paper-boxes', 'blog-paper-dieline',
        'Use the assembled structure to guide panel order, folds and the approval of printed cartons.',
        [
            'Treat the dieline as a working reference' => 'A folding carton changes from a flat blank into a three-dimensional object. The dieline identifies the cut and crease arrangement used to make that transition. Final artwork should use the approved structural layout, with its revision clearly identified, rather than a similar-looking template taken from another pack.',
            'Know the erected panel order' => 'Confirm the front, back, sides, top and bottom as they appear after assembly. A flat artwork view can make an upside-down panel surprisingly easy to overlook. Use an erected visual or a physical mock-up to review how the customer sees and opens the pack.',
            'Respect functional areas' => 'Cuts, creases, flap overlaps and joining areas have practical roles. Follow the agreed artwork guidance for clearances and coverage instead of treating every part of the flat blank as a decorative canvas. Important wording should remain readable in the assembled state and should not rely on a flap staying perfectly flat.',
            'Check the product fit independently' => 'A convincing rendering cannot prove that the product fits. Review internal dimensions, any insert, the packing method and the opening clearance with representative contents. If the product dimensions or weight change, revisit the structural brief rather than updating only the front-panel wording.',
            'Separate approvals' => 'Structural acceptance, artwork approval and regulated content review are distinct decisions. Record who owns each one and which file revision was approved. If a structural change affects a printed panel, send it back through the relevant artwork review instead of assuming the old approval still applies.',
            'Build a usable handover' => 'Provide the product details, accepted structure, artwork revision, quantity and any finishing questions. The folding-carton, tuck-end and auto-bottom guides offer different starting points for the structure discussion. Amalez PackTrade can review a paper-box inquiry without treating a generic template as a finished specification.',
        ],
        'Can a similar carton template be used as final artwork?', 'Use it only as a concept reference until the actual structural layout and dimensions have been confirmed.',
        ['Dielines', 'Folding cartons']],
    ['paper-box-finish-brief', 'Matte, gloss and detail: write a useful paper-box finish brief.', 'paper-boxes', 'blog-paper-finishes',
        'Describe the intended visual effect while keeping material, function and availability in view.',
        [
            'Describe the effect before naming the process' => 'A finish brief is clearer when it explains what the brand wants the customer to notice. Is the intention a quiet matte surface, a contrast between selected areas or a tactile emphasis? A reference image can start that conversation, but it does not establish which process produced the effect or whether it suits the proposed board.',
            'Connect the finish with the material' => 'The printed face, board and finishing treatment act together. Review how colour and fine detail look on the intended material rather than assuming that an effect on one carton transfers unchanged to another. Any finish should be confirmed with the project specification and an appropriate reference.',
            'Mark the areas clearly' => 'Where a finish is selective, identify the intended area in a controlled artwork layer or separate approved guide. Keep that guide aligned to the final dieline. Explain which elements are decorative and which must remain readable or unobstructed for practical use.',
            'Check folds and handling' => 'An attractive flat sample may not show the effect of creasing, assembly and repeated handling. Ask what sample review is appropriate for the proposed finish and structure. Packaging should still open, close and display the information required for the product.',
            'Avoid automatic environmental claims' => 'A paper-based box is not a complete environmental assessment. Coatings, laminates, adhesives, inks and the actual disposal route all matter. Do not add a recyclable, compostable or other environmental claim solely because the underlying substrate is paperboard.',
            'Keep the quotation specific' => 'List the intended material, artwork, quantity and finish areas, then distinguish essential requirements from preferences. The paper-box and sleeve guides can help identify where the printed surface appears on the pack. Confirm availability and commercial details rather than assuming a catalogue of special effects is already offered.',
        ],
        'Does a photograph prove a particular finish is available?', 'No. Use it as a visual reference and ask for the actual process, material compatibility and availability to be confirmed.',
        ['Print finishes', 'Paperboard packaging']],
    ['corrugated-box-fit-brief', 'Start a corrugated box inquiry with the packed product.', 'corrugated-boxes', 'blog-corrugated-fit',
        'Dimensions, arrangement and handling explain more than a request for a certain ply count.',
        [
            'Define the packed arrangement' => 'Begin with what goes into the box: the number of items, their dimensions, weight and sensitive surfaces. Include any primary pack and proposed insert. A loose product dimension is not the same as the space needed for the complete packed arrangement.',
            'Distinguish internal and external dimensions' => 'Internal dimensions concern fit; external dimensions affect handling, storage and transport. State which measurements are being supplied and the order used to describe them. If clearances are uncertain, say so rather than building a quotation around an unexplained allowance.',
            'Describe the journey' => 'Explain how the box will be packed, stored, stacked and distributed. Manual handling, parcel networks and grouped shipment arrangements can create different requirements. Avoid reducing the entire brief to a request for a stronger box without describing the damage concern or the conditions to be reviewed.',
            'Look beyond ply count' => 'Ply count describes part of the construction, not the performance of every possible box. Board properties, dimensions, style, closure and the contents all influence the evaluation. A divider may organise products without providing the cushioning a fragile item needs. Review the whole pack rather than one component in isolation.',
            'Agree the validation needed' => 'Where protection is important, discuss suitable sample review or testing with the responsible team. Record the packed product and the conditions represented by that evaluation. No general box description should be treated as a guarantee against every transport event.',
            'Prepare the inquiry' => 'Send the packed arrangement, internal fit requirement, total weight, quantities and distribution concerns. Compare regular slotted cases with mailer and insert concepts through the service guides, then share the brief with Amalez PackTrade. Final board and structural requirements need project-specific confirmation.',
        ],
        'Is a higher ply count a complete strength specification?', 'No. The board properties, box size, structure, closure and intended handling must be considered together.',
        ['Corrugated specifications', 'Product protection']],
    ['corrugated-print-kraft-white', 'Printing on corrugated boxes: plan for the liner and the opening.', 'corrugated-boxes', 'blog-corrugated-print',
        'Connect exterior identification and interior presentation with the real printed surface.',
        [
            'Decide what the print must communicate' => 'A shipping mark, a retail graphic and an interior brand reveal do different jobs. Define the primary purpose before adding artwork to every panel. A useful box can balance clear identification with a memorable design without sacrificing the information needed during packing and handling.',
            'Consider the liner as a colour background' => 'Natural kraft and white-faced surfaces create different starting points for printed colour and contrast. Review the design on the intended surface. A bright screen mock-up on white is not a dependable guide to the appearance of the same image on brown board.',
            'Use the correct inside and outside views' => 'Mailer panels fold and overlap. Identify the printed side and the erected orientation on the artwork layout. The inside lid can provide a deliberate graphic moment, but it should remain coherent when the contents and insert cover part of the design.',
            'Protect practical information' => 'Leave space for the actual closure and any delivery or identification labels. Ask where tape or other materials may cover the graphics. A beautiful central wordmark is less useful if it disappears under the agreed closure arrangement.',
            'Review the complete printed pack' => 'Assess readability, panel orientation and the opening sequence on a representative assembled box. Consider where surfaces may rub during handling and whether printed internal areas contact the product. Any direct-contact or resistance requirement needs an appropriate technical review rather than a visual assumption.',
            'Create a clear print brief' => 'Provide the chosen structure, liner appearance, artwork views, intended print areas and quantity. The inside-printed mailer guide can help with the opening experience, while regular slotted cases may prioritise exterior identification. Amalez PackTrade reviews these requirements without claiming a universal print specification or facility ownership.',
        ],
        'Can the same colour reference be assumed identical on kraft and white board?', 'No. The surface affects the perceived result. Agree the appropriate reference and review for the actual liner and print requirements.',
        ['Corrugated printing', 'Brand presentation']],
];
